-          "quote": "Let a finite discounted MDP have $0\\le\\gamma<1$ and terminal set $S_\\dagger$. Require episodes to reach $S_\\dagger$ in finite time almost surely from every state under each policy used. For on-policy prediction, generate independent episodes under a stationary target policy $\\pi$. In episode $i$, let $I_i(s)=1$ if $s$ is visited before termination and $I_i(s)=0$ otherwise. When $I_i(s)=1$, let $t_i(s)$ be the first visit time and let $G_{t_i(s)}$ be the discounted return from that time to termination. Maintain the visit count $C(s)$ and estimate $V^\\pi(s)$ by the sample mean of these first-visit returns: after the $m$th observed first visit to $s$, update\n\n$$\nC(s)\\leftarrow m,\\qquad V(s)\\leftarrow V(s)+\\frac{G_{t_i(s)}-V(s)}{m}.\n$$\n\nFor off-policy prediction, let episodes instead be generated under a stationary behavior policy $\\mu$, while $\\pi$ remains the target policy. Require $\\pi(a\\mid x)>0\\Rightarrow\\mu(a\\mid x)>0$ for every nonterminal state $x$ and action $a\\in A(x)$. For each first visit to $s$, set\n\n$$\nW_i(s)=\\prod_{k=t_i(s)}^{T_i-1}\\frac{\\pi(A_k\\mid S_k)}{\\mu(A_k\\mid S_k)},\n$$\n\nwhere $T_i$ is the terminal time and an empty product is $1$. After $n$ episodes, the ordinary first-visit estimator is\n\n$$\n\\widehat V^{\\rm ordinary}_n(s)=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n I_i(s)W_i(s)G_{t_i(s)}}{\\sum_{i=1}^n I_i(s)}\n$$\n\nwhen the denominator is positive. The weighted first-visit estimator is\n\n$$\n\\widehat V^{\\rm weighted}_n(s)=\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n I_i(s)W_i(s)G_{t_i(s)}}{\\sum_{i=1}^n I_i(s)W_i(s)}\n$$\n\nwhen its denominator is positive; if that denominator is zero, no weighted estimate is produced. The finite-episode likelihood-ratio identity gives the ordinary weighted return conditional mean $V^\\pi(s)$ for each positive-probability first-visit history. The self-normalized weighted estimator need not be unbiased at a finite sample size. These procedures specify estimators only and make no convergence claim or Monte Carlo control claim.",
+          "quote": "**Inputs:** A finite MDP with nonempty finite state set $S$, discount $0\\le\\gamma<1$, terminal set $S_\\dagger\\subseteq S$, an initial-state rule, and an episode budget $N\\in\\mathbb N\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. Assume the Axiom of Choice for the policy path laws defining $V^\\pi$ and the episode distributions, and that episodes reach $S_\\dagger$ in finite time almost surely from every state under each stationary policy used: the target $\\pi$, and also the behavior $\\mu$ in off-policy mode. Choose one mode: (a) on-policy prediction with independent episodes under a stationary target policy $\\pi$; or (b) off-policy prediction with independent episodes under a stationary behavior policy $\\mu$ and stationary target policy $\\pi$. In off-policy mode require $\\pi(a\\mid x)>0\\Rightarrow\\mu(a\\mid x)>0$ for every nonterminal state $x$ and action $a\\in A(x)$.\n\n**Output:** After every finite number $n$ of completed episodes, for every nonterminal state its visit count and each available estimate; report no estimate when its required denominator is zero. A finite budget $N$ returns these outputs after episode $N$; budget $\\infty$ emits successive finite-prefix outputs without a final output or convergence assertion. Set the value estimate of every terminal state to $0$.\n\nInitialize visit counts $C(s)=0$ and leave each nonterminal state's estimate unavailable. Generate successive independent episodes using the selected mode and initial-state rule; stop after $N$ episodes when $N$ is finite. In episode $i$, for each nonterminal state $s$ visited before termination, let $t_i(s)$ be its first visit time and let $G_{t_i(s)}=\\sum_{k=t_i(s)}^{T_i-1}\\gamma^{k-t_i(s)}R_{k+1}$ be the return from that visit.\n\nIn on-policy mode, update only on this first visit: set $m=C(s)+1$; if $m=1$, set $\\widehat V(s)=G_{t_i(s)}$, and otherwise set\n\n$$\n\\widehat V(s)\\leftarrow\\widehat V(s)+\\frac{G_{t_i(s)}-\\widehat V(s)}{m}.\n$$\n\nThen set $C(s)=m$. In off-policy mode, for each first visit compute\n\n$$\nW_i(s)=\\prod_{k=t_i(s)}^{T_i-1}\\frac{\\pi(A_k\\mid S_k)}{\\mu(A_k\\mid S_k)},\n$$\n\nwhere $T_i$ is the terminal time and an empty product is $1$. Maintain $B(s)=0$ and $W_\\Sigma(s)=0$ initially; add $W_i(s)G_{t_i(s)}$ to $B(s)$ and $W_i(s)$ to $W_\\Sigma(s)$, and increment $C(s)$. After any $n$ completed episodes, the ordinary first-visit estimator is\n\n$$\n\\widehat V^{\\rm ordinary}_n(s)=\\frac{B(s)}{C(s)}\n$$\n\nwhen $C(s)>0$; otherwise no ordinary estimate is produced. The weighted first-visit estimator is\n\n$$\n\\widehat V^{\\rm weighted}_n(s)=\\frac{B(s)}{W_\\Sigma(s)}\n$$\n\nwhen $W_\\Sigma(s)>0$; otherwise no weighted estimate is produced. Under target support, for each positive-probability behavior history ending immediately upon a first visit to a nonterminal state $s$, before its action is sampled, the ordinary weighted return has conditional mean $V^\\pi(s)$ by the finite-episode likelihood-ratio identity. The self-normalized weighted estimator need not be unbiased at a finite sample size. These procedures make no convergence claim or Monte Carlo control claim.",
